    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "definition",
     "title": "Scale",
     "md": "A **scale** tells how much the real distance is shrunk on the map. A scale of **1 cm to 50 km** means that 1 cm on the map is 50 km on the ground. To find a real distance, **measure** the map distance in centimetres and **multiply** by the scale: 4.5 cm on the map is 4.5 × 50 = **225 km**. A **scale bar** shows the same idea."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "definition",
     "title": "Equator and meridian",
     "md": "The **Equator** is an imaginary line round the middle of the Earth. It divides the world into the **Northern and Southern hemispheres**. The **Prime Meridian** runs from north to south through Greenwich in London; it divides the world into the **Eastern and Western hemispheres**. **Latitude** measures north or south of the Equator; **longitude** measures east or west of the Prime Meridian."
    },
